  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 114 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nMonday through Thursday:\n\nWith zonal flow aloft into late in the week, there will be\nmultiple shortwave troughs moving through the flow. This will\nprovide many opportunities for rain and thunderstorms to develop\nacross the Great Lakes Region. Mid level moisture will be\npersistent across Wisconsin with PWAT values around 1.5 to 2\ninches for the first half of the week (roughly through Tuesday).\nHeavy rain will be possible and with repeated round of storms\nthere could be some localized issues with flooding. This lines\nup with the WPC excessive rainfall outlooks for southern\nWisconsin. Mostly urban and small stream type impacts for any \nareas that receive repeated rainfall and training storms is\nexpected. The strengths of each round of storms will depend \nlargely on the timing for any diurnal instability or presence \nof an LLJ. Overall expecting afternoon/evening rounds of storms \nto be stronger than those overnight or in the early mornings. \nNow thats a bit of a generalization as persistent cloud cover or\nan environment overworked to early in the morning will leave an\nafternoon/evening round weak unless WAA can overcome that. The \nreason for all the uncertainty is due to the zonal flow. There \nare multiple difference scenarios based on the timing of \nshortwave troughs. This uncertainty in the strength and timing \nof the shortwaves will dictate storm timing and available lift.\nWhile this unsettled pattern lingers through the entire \nextended, its not anticipated for everyday to be a complete \nwashout. Its also possible that if guidance continues to have \nmajor discrepancies that the forecast holds POPs on longer than\nit should (even in the short term) just due to the amount of \nuncertainties. A real moment to wait until you can see the \nwhites of its eyes scenario. \n\nPatterson",
